{
  "abstain": false,
  "ranked_resource_ids": ["R02", "R12", "R17"],
  "answer": "R02 (Mechanics & Materials I) directly matches the query. Its description explicitly states it provides an introduction to the mechanics of solids, covering the geometry of deformation, internal forces, and material properties that quantify force-deformation relations. The course topics include Solid Mechanics and Materials Science and Engineering, confirming coverage of structural deformation, internal forces, and material properties.",
  "cited_resource_ids": ["R02"],
  "evidence_quotes": [
    {
      "resource_id": "R02",
      "quote": "This course provides an introduction to the mechanics of solids with applications to science and engineering. It emphasizes three essential features of mechanics analyses: the geometry of motion and/or deformation of structures and conditions of geometric fit; the forces on and within structures and assemblages; and physical aspects of structural systems, including material properties, that quantify relations between forces and motions or deformation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "abstain": false,
  "ranked_resource_ids": ["R10", "R08", "R06"],
  "answer": "The query seeks an introductory course linking gene structure and protein synthesis to cells and organisms. R10 is the best match because it explicitly covers the fundamental principles of biochemistry, genetics, molecular biology, and cell biology, with emphasis on gene structure/regulation and protein synthesis, and how these integrate into cells and multicellular organisms. R08 (Genetics) covers gene structure and function at molecular, cellular, and organismal levels but is narrower in scope. R06 (Principles of Chemical Science) includes macromolecular assembly such as DNA but does not emphasize cellular/organismal integration.",
  "cited_resource_ids": ["R10"],
  "evidence_quotes": [
    {
      "resource_id": "R10",
      "quote": "covers the same core material, which includes the fundamental principles of biochemistry, genetics, molecular biology, and cell biology"
    },
    {
      "resource_id": "R10",
      "quote": "covers the structure and regulation of genes, as well as the structure and synthesis of proteins, how these molecules are integrated into cells, and how these cells are integrated into multicellular systems and organisms"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "abstain": false,
  "ranked_resource_ids": ["R10", "R08", "R06"],
  "answer": "The query asks for an introductory course that covers how gene structure and protein synthesis relate to cells and organisms. R10 (7.012 Introduction to Biology) explicitly states that its core material covers \"the structure and regulation of genes, as well as the structure and synthesis of proteins, how these molecules are integrated into cells, and how these cells are integrated into multicellular systems and organisms.\" This directly matches the request. R08 (7.03 Genetics) is a strong second choice because it covers \"structure and function of genes\" and \"use of genetic methods to analyze protein function\" at the level of molecules, cells, and multicellular organisms. R06 (5.111SC Principles of Chemical Science) is a weaker but relevant third option as it includes \"the assembly of macromolecules as in polymers and DNA\" and relationships between chemistry and biology.",
  "cited_resource_ids": ["R10"],
  "evidence_quotes": [
    {
      "resource_id": "R10",
      "quote": "covers the structure and regulation of genes, as well as the structure and synthesis of proteins, how these molecules are integrated into cells, and how these cells are integrated into multicellular systems and organisms"
    }
  ]
}

